{
  "id": "u06-l04",
  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "This one, those ones",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**এই** (*ei*) 'this' and **ওই** (*oi*) 'that' go before a noun: **এই দোকানদার** 'this shopkeeper'. On their own, add **-টা**: **এইটা** 'this one', **ওইটা** 'that one' — perfect for pointing at a pile of fruit.\n\nThe classifier **-টা** on a noun makes it specific, like 'the': **মাছটা** 'the fish (that one)'. **-গুলো** makes it specific *and* plural: **কলাগুলো** 'the bananas', **এগুলো** 'these', **ওগুলো** 'those' (you'll also hear the more emphatic এইগুলো / ওইগুলো).\n\nIn speech you'll often hear **এই মাছটা** — both 'this' and -টা together. That's normal, not redundant.\n\n'Not this one, that one' is simply **এইটা না, ওইটা।**",
  "vocab": [
